WEBVTT

00:00:01.170 --> 00:00:24.818
So everyone, I welcome you all. And today I want to do something very special because in the past I purely talked about Jung and Jung's concepts. But the best way to learn something and to understand something is to have examples and to have some reference points, find what Jung tries to get at.

00:00:25.363 --> 00:00:45.418
Otherwise it can be incredibly dry and it can be really, really dry when you read the books. Even though he has a lot of examples. So I wanted to do something today by analyzing a movie where I think there are a lot of examples in it that can help to understand some key concepts about Jung.

00:00:46.222 --> 00:01:10.086
So this is not saying that Barbie, the movie, is necessarily a Jungian movie that somebody set out to say, okay, I'm trying to put in as much concepts of Jung as possible into there, which would be interesting, but it would surprise me if this is how the movie got created. But I saw the movie.

00:01:10.585 --> 00:01:27.971
I really liked the movie. I thought it was really, really entertaining. And the more I thought about it, the more concepts of Jung I found that I can use as examples. So this is not to say this is a Jungian movie and every Jungian has to see this movie. But rather, there's a lot of concepts in there.

00:01:28.597 --> 00:01:51.481
And the way how I want to structure is I want to tell a little bit first about the concepts. So everybody who saw the movie already can maybe start already to think, oh, okay, they might get where I'm trying to get it. And then I will make a summary of the movie just for those who have not seen it, maybe.

00:01:52.110 --> 00:02:16.477
And then I will go, okay, directly applying all the things from before on the movie. And there's some, some really, really cool things that took me some time to figure out. But I saw the movie twice just this weekend again and took a lot of notes. And yeah, so when we talk about Jung and his life's work.

00:02:17.737 --> 00:02:39.798
The main part where everything revolves around is this idea that you have the conscious and the unconscious and how those two interact with each other. So how do things become, come from the unconscious into consciousness and the other way around?

00:02:39.927 --> 00:02:52.640
Like how do things go into The unconscious conscious and all the other way around. And how does consciousness evolve? Because consciousness is basically us, how we are.

00:02:54.704 --> 00:03:17.629
So for him and what he found in his research and studies is that consciousness is very often associated with spiritual air. Lofty, also abstract things. So let's say things high up in the sky, removed a little bit from everything.

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:36.702
And the, the unconscious is very much earthly, down to earth. And this interaction of those and the symbolization you have in the birds as consciousness and conscious things and snakes or reptiles. As things of the earth.

00:03:37.607 --> 00:03:57.430
And this is why you have very often have this image of a bird, uh, that catches a snake, for example, or wrestles with a snake. You see that very often, like the Hunger Games. There's a new movie coming out and they have it as a front poster. And they of course did not come up with it. This is a very old symbol and it's used again and again.

00:03:58.481 --> 00:04:17.974
And for Jung, it's such a thing that Uh, symbolizes how our psyche works, that there's this constant struggle and interaction between consciousness and unconscious. So the main thing is that the unconscious constantly interferes with consciousness.

00:04:18.022 --> 00:04:42.138
So we set up a plan, we want to do something, but something comes from the side and distracts us. So the idea of the Freudian slip. For example, comes from that. We want to say one thing, but we're saying another thing because our unconscious brings us to do that. We slip, we trip, we think of something inappropriate. Just images come up when somebody tells something to us.

00:04:44.586 --> 00:05:06.701
This is the unconscious interfering with our plans and our consciousness. And consciousness, of course, does not like that. So, because the unconscious is messy, it's like the earth. It's very messy. It's all entangled. It's not very clear. It's not necessarily structured. It's nature.

00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:32.452
So the consciousness tries to repress and remove everything that does not neatly fit into consciousness, where everything is structured, is orderly and clean, and pushes it down. So everything that does not fit, for example, when we have an image of ourself, how we see ourself, everything that does not fit, we try to mute down and say, okay, this is not so important.

00:05:32.566 --> 00:05:44.899
That's not really me. It gets pushed into the unconscious, is removed from the very clean place that we have into kind of the dark and shadowy place and dirty place where we think where it belongs.

00:05:47.031 --> 00:06:08.963
And the problem that Jung saw, especially today for the modern world, where consciousness is very strong because we have the institutions and we have schoolwork, all those things that are there to train our will to power and to expand our consciousness with capabilities, is that people with their minds kind of fly away.

00:06:09.686 --> 00:06:29.517
They might think that their consciousness, uh, mind is separated from the body. It's something else. Two different things that kind of coexist, but in the end, it's, it's all the same. It's one body. It's also part of one psyche, but for consciousness, it's way easier to just think in conscious terms and to ignore the rest.

00:06:31.372 --> 00:06:56.149
And this idea of we have two poles acting against each other, Jung uses very often what he calls the opposites. And he says this, The psyche and psychic processes interacting has very often to do with opposites that are hard to combine and hard to bring together. And bringing them together is a difficult task and a lot of work.

00:06:56.711 --> 00:07:18.311
But he says, when consciousness pushes too far into one direction, it will provoke the unconscious to to counteract, to bring back a balance, to remind the person that there's more to them, that they have a complete body and a complete psyche and everything, to bring them into a higher state.

00:07:19.570 --> 00:07:38.904
Of course, this has to be a conscious decision to do so. This counterbalancing act, the unconscious does all by itself, but consciousness also has to follow that and not ignore it because then It gets worse and worse. So for these opposites, it's important because for Jung, that's really part of how we perceive the world.

00:07:39.790 --> 00:08:04.392
He says we only have dark when there's light and the other way around. We only have high when there's low. When we say something, we also talk about the opposite. He references everything against each other. The one can't be without the other. And one of the basics opposites that he sees besides consciousness and unconscious is that of Logos and Eros.

00:08:05.018 --> 00:08:29.187
Logos being the, um, function that separates and Eros being the function that binds together. So for him and very lowest, uh, of the psyche, there are these forces that are constantly interacting because this is what you also do in real life. You separate or you bring together, and this is constant.

00:08:29.251 --> 00:08:51.076
And you can't say, oh, I only separate and then everything is nice because then everything is dead, or only combine, then everything gets entangled and there's not really clear. It's like a completely dirty room. So it's both are necessary in a balance. And he sees that, uh, they're both in the psyche, but based on the gender of a person.

00:08:51.706 --> 00:09:15.595
It's rather the person feels more at home in Logos or more at home in Eros. So every person has the same, it's just in a different ratio what gets preferred. And he says for men, it's the Logos aspect, the separating aspect being preferred where people feel at home, which makes Eros, the, the unconscious side, the kind of difficult side.

00:09:16.134 --> 00:09:37.459
And for women, it's the other way around. The Eros, the combining function, the relation function is in the main view, while Logos is more in the dark. This again means this is like a starting position. It does not mean that this has to be like that, but it is how, let's say, the, the game starts.

00:09:39.446 --> 00:10:02.187
And bringing this all together and developing a whole persona and a whole psyche. Means to traverse one's own psychology, to go into the dark parts, because only there can be growth. What you know is light, lit up, everything is structured, everything is ordered, that there's no room to grow as a person. And one has a whole psyche and it has to be lived.

00:10:02.347 --> 00:10:25.154
This is what the unconscious pushes. And there's one factor on the unconscious that does that. And this is the Self. And the Self can be understood something like a future you. And it puts in all these different tasks and all the different situations to one that forces one to evolve. At least one should decide to also evolve.

00:10:25.653 --> 00:10:42.804
One can oppose, but then of course it becomes more and more difficult with the unconscious because it will interact more and more and interfere. So when we start out. An individuation process. This is called this journey through the psyche.

00:10:45.023 --> 00:11:07.938
People have a low level of consciousness and this low level of consciousness, he references to earlier societies, like that we have this very strong consciousness is relatively new for him, just a couple of thousands of years since writing starts. He says that's the beginning of consciousness. Everything before people are in an unconscious state.

00:11:08.051 --> 00:11:26.953
And he, he says he could see it in some, like, pre-scientific societies, primitive societies, he called them, hunter-gatherers, uh, who had no contact yet with, uh, the more civilized world, not being a value judgment, civilized in that sense.

00:11:28.659 --> 00:11:51.274
And he noted there that these tribes were very much afraid of everything that could interfere with consciousness because they only had very little, unstable consciousness. So they had to kind of get rid of everything that might interfere. So everything that was strange, unusual, even when people had bad luck, they tried to ostracize them.

00:11:53.512 --> 00:12:14.931
And he noted that the idea of an individual did not really exist. People existed more in a group. So they were one with other people or group or even things or places. The psychology was not separated from the surroundings. The idea of an I and of being an individual was not there.

00:12:15.336 --> 00:12:27.269
So this is a path of individuation, become an individual, something that is different from the rest, not completely separated, but, but different. And aware of that difference.

00:12:30.967 --> 00:12:51.697
And the constant journey and the constant problem of individuation is that what people already know and already can do, Jung calls as superior function. It's like you have two hands, but you like one really, really a lot more than the other. And when there's something new to do, you first try it with your strong hand.

00:12:52.881 --> 00:13:09.183
And as people might think, what happens in individuation is exactly that the Self pushes people to use the other hand also to develop it because you have two hands and you should be able to do use both in a kind of sophistication.

00:13:11.030 --> 00:13:29.531
And this means people try with all the tools, all the knowledge that they have, and they fail. Because they have to utilize the things they do not yet have. And this interaction can feel like a divine interaction. And it's what he calls the transcendent function.

00:13:29.884 --> 00:13:44.071
By you growing outside of yourself, you're transversing your own being in these situations, everything that you are, to become something more. And this is very, very interesting thoughts.

00:13:45.818 --> 00:14:07.621
And he says there are people that have different states of conscious ability and conscious sets of minds. He says just because we live in modernity does not mean everybody is modern, that everybody has like the newest version of consciousness. He says in society you have different spheres.

00:14:08.281 --> 00:14:35.606
And even back then he said, okay, when I want to meet primitive people, I ride the tram in Zürich because you have people in his view that are still living in the Middle Ages or the, the ancient Greek in those times, just a different way of looking at the world, seeing the world, and a different way how much is integrated of all the functions and capabilities there are.

00:14:35.734 --> 00:14:54.134
He also says there's like these people that live in the the year 3000 already. And he says this can make people pretty lonely because there is nobody to reference towards and to interact with on, on such a level.

00:14:56.340 --> 00:15:20.419
And the last point I want to bring from Jung's ideas and concepts for exploring this movie His idea is when things come from the unconscious, consciousness does not know what to do with them. This is why consciousness is afraid of the unconscious contents because they are scary. They are hard to untangle.

00:15:20.879 --> 00:15:37.725
They're mixed up. They look dark and muddy. And he calls that archaic. So like really, really simple. Like a wild dog. It's volatile. It can be aggressive. It's scary. You don't know how to interact.

00:15:38.990 --> 00:16:01.328
But when you spend time with these contents, the consciousness kind of unpacks them and untangles them from other contents. And to stay with the example of the dog, the dog can become domesticated. And then you can interact with it. Great. You can rely on it. You can trust it.

00:16:01.553 --> 00:16:24.123
You can play with it and you can build a relationship to it. And he sees that with all the things that are in the unconscious, that they are able to build a relationship with it, even though it looks very, very hard in the beginning. And this progression from archaic to differentiated and sophisticated, this is also part of individuation.

00:16:24.253 --> 00:16:47.493
But like every part that's in the unconscious goes through this and it's again and again this pain and very hard development. So I would move to a summary of the movie now, and I wrote it down just to make it very quick and not bore anyone who already saw it, but I want to point out some key points that are important.

00:16:48.731 --> 00:17:12.927
For the discussion. So Barbie is basically having a perfect life in Barbieland, which is a fantasy playland for little girls. Everything is fine until a certain day she starts having intrusive thoughts about death and suddenly nothing works for her anymore. She's told that there's something wrong in the real world.

00:17:13.412 --> 00:17:25.036
With the person that is playing with her, and she has now to go there and fix it. And the reason why she does it is because she wants to have everything stay the same in Barbieland.

00:17:26.904 --> 00:17:50.720
And along on her journey comes Ken, who is completely engrossed by Barbie and wants to be with her all the time, and it's up to the point of obsession. And they come to the real world, but Barbie learns that everything is kind of in a mirror world. Everything is mixed and she does not know how to behave.

00:17:51.137 --> 00:18:09.573
She gets into problems with the police and Ken explores the world and gets completely wrong ideas what it means to be a man. The company that makes Barbie, Mattel, gets wind of it. They fear marketing. PR disaster, so they want to lock her up and try to catch her.

00:18:12.006 --> 00:18:25.840
And Barbie finds out the reason for her thoughts, and the reasons are that not a little girl is playing with her, but rather a grown woman. And a grown woman has a way more complex psychology than a little girl, and this is why she's now having these complex thoughts.

00:18:27.857 --> 00:18:38.215
They're getting back to Barbieland, and Ken is already there, and he overturned society and turned it in kind of a dude bro culture, and he brainwashed all the Barbies.

00:18:40.899 --> 00:19:05.382
And Barbie with the woman from the real world, Gloria, and all the cast-out Barbies that are in Barbieland, hatch a plan to make the Kens fight against each other. So to bring back some stability again. They succeed.

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:29.168
The dudebro culture gets dismantled, but Barbie feels and realizes that she does not belong in Barbieland anymore. So she decides to become a real woman and go into the real world. And this is a short summary. And I want to make a disclaimer, as I mentioned in the beginning, I don't see Barbie as a Jungian movie.

00:19:29.409 --> 00:19:48.505
It's just a great way to show some Jungian concepts. But what is my bridge to bring in Jung? And there's a very strong reason for that. The layer that combines or connects all human beings, it's the collective unconscious. Like we have our individual life, our individual journey.

00:19:49.650 --> 00:20:14.005
But if you go low enough in the psyche, we have enough that connects us with other people that we have the same experiences they do, even in dreams or in visions or just in, in life. And when we have art, works of art, when they are able to talk to these deep layers, they are able to talk to a huge group of people.

00:20:15.934 --> 00:20:40.559
Ah, potentially to everybody on, on planet Earth today and in the future. So this is now the most successful movie of the year, and there's only a handful of movies that earned more money than Barbie. So it's popular. So we have to assume it's archetypal. And this is why I want to talk about it because the archetypal things show us how our psyche works.

00:20:41.157 --> 00:21:04.970
Deep inside. And when we experience something like that, a great piece of art, just as an example, it talks to us on a very deep level and we're engrossed by it and we want to spend time with it and we want to understand it. And if it happens to enough people and they're willing to spend money, then movie studios or songmakers or whoever can make a lot of money.

00:21:06.603 --> 00:21:29.057
My personal view on the movie is, is there's a huge, huge disconnect between what is said and what is shown. So very often the things that are said don't make sense in the context where they are presented, and that makes it a difficult movie to get a hard grasp on it. But I will get to that later in the end.

00:21:30.562 --> 00:21:55.338
So to, to analyze that, And to bring this now together, the concepts of Jung and Barbie the movie with the summary, we have to look at Barbieland, which is this fantasy land where basically the first act of the movie is taking place. And the most important thing to understand there is that it's, it's not the real world.

00:21:55.402 --> 00:22:18.074
It's not even comparable. It's. Sterile in that sense. There is no flowing water. There are no living animals. There is a day and night cycle that seems to be the only one, which is kind of shows the passage of time. There is no birth. There's no death. There's no aging.

00:22:18.589 --> 00:22:42.680
There are not even relationships. The cars don't have motors. And very often they don't even need to drive the cars. And they have nothing that really breaks. Nobody gets sick. Nobody gets ill. It's every day the best day, every day. And nobody is really concerned about it.

00:22:42.760 --> 00:23:00.004
I don't know if you've seen the movie Groundhog Day with Bill Murray, but he's also reliving the same day every day. And it takes him just a couple of turns that he tries to kill himself all the time. Because real human beings, they can't do it. They can't stand it to have this constant repetition. It does not matter how great it is.

00:23:00.068 --> 00:23:18.815
When it's always great, it gets unbearable anyways. It's the same as trying to lie down on the most comfortable couch that you can think of, or most comfortable bed. Even after a couple of minutes trying to not to move, it becomes agony because human beings have to move. They have to change. They have to— something has to happen.

00:23:20.133 --> 00:23:44.319
And this has to be kept in mind because all the things that are happening there and people are doing, it's all play. It's make pretend. It's a charade. They drive around with trash cans and I'm pretty sure there's nothing in it because nobody's eating anything. The container with milk or what it is, it's empty. There's no waste.

00:23:44.896 --> 00:24:06.726
Nobody goes to the toilet. There is really nothing. In that sense, it's incredibly, incredibly sterile. But it's not only that Barbieland is strange and in a kind of heightened reality. The same is for the real world. The real world also is more like a parody.

00:24:07.569 --> 00:24:28.810
When they go there and they interact with the people there, they also act very strange. It's like, it's just the opposite of Barbieland. The opposite of something very strange is again something strange. They go to the construction site and immediately it's just one sexist sentence after the other. And no real person will talk like this, especially not in this situation.

00:24:29.147 --> 00:24:43.007
It's a comedy, it's heightened reality. It's more similar to like a Wes Anderson movie where it's also childlike. The Barbieland is childlike because it's a fantasy of small children.

00:24:43.760 --> 00:25:04.895
But as we learned during the movie, it's not only the fantasy of small children, but rather of guys running a company thinking of a place of Barbieland. Like the whole board, they're all men and they think about, okay, what this female society could look like.

00:25:05.698 --> 00:25:27.682
And if you checked out the quote for this event, I reference this, where Jung says about the problem with men looking at women and trying to understand the psychology is that they projecting their own inferior femininity into that and they see that. And that makes it then complicated to find the right interaction.

00:25:28.292 --> 00:25:52.016
So this is why nobody thinks about Ken, because the little girls, they're not grown enough. To think about boys and the guys, of course, do not care about the boys in that land. So this is why the Kens don't have any homes. And this childlike quality shows itself in the people as they have, they have no filter, right? They just say what comes to mind. They're very naive.

00:25:52.387 --> 00:26:13.834
So in Barbieland, it's okay, but in the real world, they would get into trouble because they're thinking, okay, you don't have to pay. You can tell people whatever. It's outrageous. And that's what's funny. It's a kind of a fish out of water story. The things they do are outrageous and that makes it a comedy. Also in Barbieland, they know things, but it's only book knowledge.

00:26:14.412 --> 00:26:28.228
It's definitions. And that's enough because it's make pretend. Like people pretend to be a doctor, people pretend to be president, but there's no need for a president or a doctor. So it's enough when you wear the right clothes and talk in that way.

00:26:30.090 --> 00:26:47.833
And this is in one of the funniest situations when Barbie is accused of being a fascist and she just says, uh, people think I'm controlling the railways and the flow of commerce because that's, yeah, that's one definition of fascism. But this is of course not meant and this is completely beside the point.

00:26:48.575 --> 00:27:08.374
But this is this idea of you have this completely disembodied knowledge that is separated from a strong underpinning, is not connected to anything. She maybe knows the textbook definition of fascism, but certainly she does not know about Nazi Germany or fascist Italy and the Holocaust and all those things.

00:27:11.559 --> 00:27:34.944
This is an important point to understand that the Barbies and Kens, they're not really men and women. In their minds, they are, they have these things, but bodily and everything else, there's no need for such a separation between men and women because there's not really a problem to solve this. This is not nature. It's completely unnatural.

00:27:36.130 --> 00:27:50.081
So this is a state of Barbieland in the beginning of the movie. And this is a place where Barbie grows up and exists. And the way how her story starts is by having these thoughts of death.

00:27:50.809 --> 00:28:13.650
And this is the interesting thing when you want to look at it from a Jungian perspective, because when we say she's a, has a female psychology, of course the Logos part would be inferior in her, and this would be the things that come through the unconscious. And the Logos is associated with words. This means she having thoughts of death.

00:28:14.324 --> 00:28:38.901
This is her Logos coming up and presenting us something from the unconscious, which is completely strange and foreign to her, but she's so naive that she does not realize it and blurts it out in a party and everybody around her is mortified by what she said. And it's only then that she realizes there's something wrong. And this is when things start to fail for her.

00:28:38.982 --> 00:29:02.043
Like all the things we have seen before, one of those perfect days, suddenly she wakes up, has bad breath, the water has the wrong temperature, the milk is old, the toast is burnt. All the things she never had to think about, suddenly they become problematic. Suddenly she can't live her normal life anymore. She falls from the roof. And this is what makes her concerned.

00:29:03.538 --> 00:29:28.398
And one of the coolest things that I realized is this, that her feet are flat. The feet are always like this. They're always suspended in the air because of the high heels. But once the unconscious comes up and like the bodily comes, she gets into contact with the full earth with her feet and she does not know how to deal with it and how to walk.

00:29:29.710 --> 00:29:54.254
And this reminded me of something that Jung said about patients he had, British soldiers who were stationed in India. And he said, if you go to a completely different culture from yours, it's kind of your psyche trying to protect itself and becoming more stereotypical in the way you are and where you're from.

00:29:54.987 --> 00:30:19.666
And this is an effort to not touch ground on the foreign soil and not let it infect the person. And he noted that very often when people, for example, went to Africa and India and were there for a long time, that kind of the psychology of the soil creeps up in their unconscious and starts to influencing them. And people are trying to shut themselves off.

00:30:20.900 --> 00:30:32.335
So this image that he had of the feet of the soldiers or other people kind of hovering above the ground, this is now what's happening to Barbie.

00:30:32.609 --> 00:30:53.551
She's full feet on the ground and it's her body trying to bring her more down to the earthly, out of the abstract, the sterile and dead, into the nourishing and also rejuvenating, renovating earth.

00:30:55.839 --> 00:31:12.856
So what does Barbie do? And this is something that happens very, very often in movies. Normally also in fairy tales, people go to a witch. They don't know what to do, so they don't turn to normal society. They go to the outcast of society and ask, okay, what's wrong?

00:31:13.459 --> 00:31:38.319
In this case, it's Weird Barbie, and Weird Barbie has special knowledge because she got through the wringer of the real world. They're like, the real world interacted too much with her and now she's damaged goods. And this is why the society puts her as an outcast outside of the city. And even her house looks weird. And she has superior knowledge above the other Barbies.

00:31:38.730 --> 00:31:53.163
Because the other Barbies, they just shun her. They ignore her and talk behind her back, and she has to live alone in her house. But if things are serious and people can't function normally anymore, they go to the witch and ask her for advice.

00:31:54.841 --> 00:32:19.050
And she tells Barbie that somebody is playing with her and something's wrong with that person. And that's why She's having problems. And this is so cool because Barbie does not know that somebody is playing with her. And it's this idea of psychoanalysis, this is one of the staples of psychoanalysis, that you are not the lord in your own house.

00:32:19.692 --> 00:32:37.000
There are also other guests and they push and pull you and influence you. So it's her unconscious acting up and she realizes, oh, okay, I have to now go into the place and through the dark of the unconscious to find out what is wrong.

00:32:37.674 --> 00:32:57.629
So it's of course an outward journey as she goes to another place, but it's really an inward journey to find out more. All she has is the thought of death and her life is not working anymore. And she wants to find out why. Well, basically she does not want to find out why. The reason why she's doing is because she does not want things to change.

00:32:58.210 --> 00:33:14.757
She only wants to interact to the point where it's gone and she can ignore it and live the life again and have the best day of her life again and again. So coming back to a prior primal ideal state.

00:33:18.022 --> 00:33:31.457
Yeah, I mentioned already that she's incredibly childlike and naive with the people around her. She thinks that this girl, Sasha, is playing with her because she has the visions. She's turning inward.

00:33:31.521 --> 00:33:52.434
She does a little bit of active imagination, trying to find out what she sees in her own unconscious, and she sees that little girl and thinks, okay, this is the person I need to talk to. But of course she does it in the completely wrong way because she does not know how to interact with real people. And this puts her completely into disarray and she cries.

00:33:54.590 --> 00:34:15.881
But something very interesting happens is when she's in the real world and when she's sitting alone on that bench and trying to think, uh, using her, let's say, inferior functions of the unconscious to come up with a solution, she sees all the different people and she sees an old lady.

00:34:15.945 --> 00:34:40.167
And the first time that she's seen an old lady, She sees death and decay and an end coming. Because in Barbieland, everything is plastic, everything is fantastic. Nothing really ends. It's the best day every day. But there she sees people crying, people laughing, things living, changing. And this has an influence on her and she cries and she cries for the first time of her life.

00:34:40.231 --> 00:35:04.265
She explores new different parts of herself. And her experience, and she likes it. And this is a very, very important point, especially for the end of the movie. And how she tries to interact with Sasha is in this very relation-based mode of being. This is— she's friendly with everyone, right?

00:35:04.489 --> 00:35:20.920
