00:39:32.192 --> 00:39:52.931
This is what Carl Gustav Jung spent his life on by saying, okay, we have this ancient wisdom of the psyche and mythology and fairy tales and religion and all these things, but it's not available for us anymore. We're too removed. We now have other conscious needs. So it's not there for us. And we have to explore it new.

00:39:53.076 --> 00:40:15.253
And his idea was that psychology was a way to explore these things again and to bring them back into culture. But you cannot say today, okay, I'm gonna go back into Christianity and live it the same way how it was lived 500, 1,000, or 2,000 years ago. It's just not possible for us. We have to find a different way and move forward.

00:40:15.638 --> 00:40:33.678
It doesn't mean that it's useless, but it has to be rediscovered in a new way. The old ways, the symbols, the dogmas, and so on, they don't work anymore. So we need new ones. And this is a journey that Barbie is taking, and I can't believe that I'm talking like this about a movie about a doll that's being sold to little girls.

00:40:35.520 --> 00:40:52.453
Parallel to that, you have Ken's journey, and this is also very interesting because you might remember what I said about you have Barbie and her thing coming from the unconscious are intrusive thoughts. For Ken, it's intrusive feelings. It's the Eros function. It's a relation function.

00:40:53.080 --> 00:41:17.221
But the same way how Barbie can't deal with the thoughts of death, he can't deal with feeling because he gets an obsession with Barbie and he is completely focused on her and he only wants to be with her and she does not understand it. He very also does not understand it, but he just wants to be with her all the time. So they're in a similar situation.

00:41:17.335 --> 00:41:42.198
There's something inside them. That starts bugging them and they have to find a way how to deal with it. The problem for Ken is at least Barbie found a real person in the real world, like an ambassador who can explain how the real world works and what it means to be a real woman. And Ken does not have that.

00:41:42.606 --> 00:42:02.318
And this is very interesting because he's in a, in the Logos sphere. He's in the word sphere, right? He's in the separation sphere. So he gets into the real world and what does he do? He walks around alone. He watches people. He watches people in a gym. He sees some advertisement and the next thing he says, like, I want to learn more.

00:42:02.815 --> 00:42:22.481
I'm going to a library and get books and read about it. I'm going to read about horses and trucks and all those things. And He's not well enough versed in the real world to understand what all these things mean. It's all really just words on paper. It's only abstract again.

00:42:23.252 --> 00:42:37.369
And there's not one male person that he really builds a connection with who can explain to him what is necessary. He would have needed a father to explain what it means to be a man, but rather he is a naive child.

00:42:37.627 --> 00:42:54.604
Goes out to the real world, sees all the flashy things, the big cars, the leather fur, the loudness, and just wants to copy it like an adolescent boy who does not understand what it really means to be a man. It's because it's all show. It's all charade.

00:42:55.828 --> 00:43:12.557
And he then thinks, and this is, this is really the thing that he's projecting his insecurities. Into the outside world, sees those and thinks this is real. And he thinks, oh, all you need to be is be a man and suddenly you get everything.

00:43:13.752 --> 00:43:30.613
But he finds out, oh no, you need to all those stuff, you need to change, you need to get education, certification, training, right? And he says, oh no, that's, that's way too hard. I don't want to change. That's so difficult.

00:43:31.194 --> 00:43:54.315
Okay, I will set up a new society somewhere else where I have all these great things that I just saw and that I think that are amazing, but I don't have to do really the effort. So rather than confronting his own soul and own being and questioning that, he tries to change the world. In a Jungian sense and Jungian concept, that's possession and inflation.

00:43:54.852 --> 00:44:16.950
So There's certain forces in his unconscious that are taking over and bringing him to force himself onto other people with his views and his ideas. And he's shaping the world. There's this quote of Dostoevsky that fits perfectly, that people rather change the world than confront their own soul.

00:44:17.079 --> 00:44:36.864
And A simple reason why he is amazed by horses. He's never seen real animals. There are no real elements in, in Barbieland. So the first and only animal that he sees is a horse.

00:44:37.641 --> 00:44:58.357
And you have to get into the mind of somebody who has never seen any animal and then suddenly sees one. Of course, they're amazed. Of course, they're completely transfixed about it. And that's why he thinks, okay, they are having, sharing the same space as human beings. So they have to be as important and as involved as everybody else.

00:44:58.438 --> 00:45:16.964
But this is again, his complete misunderstanding about everything of the real world and just bringing selective, very shiny things. He's engrossed by the aesthetics, but he does not get the meaning and the underlying things. Like you can't work out how society works by watching people training in a gym.

00:45:17.478 --> 00:45:39.527
But then you have again this heightened reality where they call themselves, you're the man and man, man, man. So that's the funny aspect of the movie. He has also a little arc in the movie in the sense that he can bring his relationship function, relational function under control and integrate it.

00:45:40.053 --> 00:46:03.849
By burying the hatchet with the rivalry with the other Ken. So in the beginning, it's really this rivalry between these two and up to an armed conflict in the end, but they can find commonality and then be friends. So this is his journey coming to terms with that.

00:46:04.203 --> 00:46:23.627
But yeah, he's a little boy who does not understand. Anything. How Barbieland evolves over the movie is also very, very interesting. I mentioned in the beginning how in Barbieland everything is very spiritual, abstract, a little bit like consciousness, and that sense it's rootless.

00:46:25.482 --> 00:46:43.617
And what Jung mentions very often, when people lack this connection to nature and the body and the unconscious, They can be pushed around like a balloon filled with air. There's nothing keeping them really down. They can move here, they can move there. There's nothing anchoring them.

00:46:44.662 --> 00:47:00.925
So the example that they bring in the movie is that of the smallpox that killed countless people in the Americas. Like, the Europeans had very strong immune system by living so close to certain animals for a very long time in cities.

00:47:01.423 --> 00:47:25.752
And they brought all those infectious diseases to North America where there were no immunity and no health supporting systems that could help them survive it. And it was a crazy time. Within 100 years, 90% of the people died and with more time, even more. And the idea is here the same.

00:47:26.220 --> 00:47:45.959
You have all these infectious things from the real world, the same as this infected Barbie and also like Ken, suddenly coming to everyone. And this is why he can so easily convince the Barbies to go along with his completely new opposite culture, because there's nothing grounding them. They're getting programmed.

00:47:47.162 --> 00:48:07.296
Before, they have been unconscious, and afterwards they've been unconscious, but they've been directed into new direction. And the thing you have to think about when they deprogram the Barbies, All they do is just reprogram them to something else. They're still in this very unconscious state in the sense that they don't have really contact with the real world.

00:48:07.927 --> 00:48:27.252
They're just being told things again. So it's easy to change their minds because there's nothing behind it. What's interesting also is you have this separation in the society between like the regular Barbies and the regular Kens, and then you have the outcasts. You have like Allan, there's only one Allan.

00:48:27.819 --> 00:48:50.433
And he's also on a higher sphere of consciousness as he realizes that something is strange. And this is why he also wants to escape because he says, this is not where I belong. I should be somewhere else. And he does not have the chance. He has to stay. But who helps in the end to deprogram or reprogram all the Barbies? It's all the outcasts. It's the Weird Barbie.

00:48:51.216 --> 00:48:59.822
The ones with weird functions that got off the market, like the pregnant Barbie. They just put her in a fence so that she can't go anywhere and can't do anything.

00:49:00.112 --> 00:49:23.992
So they separate all the strange things, but the strange things are in the end what saves Barbieland because it's the unusual things, the unfitting things, uh, muddy, dirty, shadowy things that you just want to repress. This is the savior. This is stepping through this shadow and coming into a higher sphere and becoming more integrated.

00:49:27.806 --> 00:49:43.469
So the society tries that, but in the end, their society is saved by that. So there's a very interesting thought. And how the Barbieland also changes through the journey of Barbie and Ken is by introducing more complex ways of being.

00:49:45.515 --> 00:50:09.059
Liking, lying, pretending, making things up, all those things shows already a more sophisticated cognitive functioning. Like very small children, they can't do it, but when children grow, they also start to learn lying and pretending and all those things. And they also do it. So there's a general evolvement of that.

00:50:09.726 --> 00:50:27.602
But in the end they say, okay, maybe things don't have to come back to the same stage as before, but in the end they're the same. They're still completely the same. The Kens still don't have anything to do in the society, and they also very gladly go into the unconscious state again.

00:50:27.955 --> 00:50:51.380
And this is one aspect where I say, okay, there's a disconnect between what is said and the context where it's said. Like the president says, oh, we don't have to go back to the way it was before. But then everything she does cements that, and people are just unconscious again. The thing is, in Barbieland, all the problems are societal because there's no nature.

00:50:52.280 --> 00:51:04.821
So they can be changed all the time without problem, without repercussion, because there's nothing anchored, there's nothing behind it, there's not anything you have to take into consideration. People don't really need each other because there are no problems. It's It's all pretend.

00:51:06.688 --> 00:51:28.064
And what people you have in society, they're doctors, presidents, Nobel Prize laureates, and so on. They have a lot of clothes. There's no sweatshop Barbie. There's no plumber Barbie. It's like everything just exists. They don't have to make anything. But of course, if you want to have something from nature, you have to really work for that. To bring that all into conclusion.

00:51:30.789 --> 00:51:55.775
To see Barbie's journey as really this Pinocchio journey of Pinocchio realizing he does not want to be a doll anymore, but taking the whole movie to learn that and having this pretty intricate journey of individuation that I found really interesting of this interaction between consciousness and unconscious and all these references between the spiritual and the earthly.

00:51:58.720 --> 00:52:16.764
All in all, I have to say it's amazing that the mother company Mattel went along with it. They even have a part in the movie, and I really wonder why they did it because it's not really— it's good Barbie advertisement, but it could have gone in a completely wrong direction. This movie was in development hell.

00:52:16.844 --> 00:52:38.029
This is the term for projects that don't make it, that are constantly being rewritten. Different people are on it so that they decided on this version. I think they at a certain point gave up and said like, okay, do whatever you want. It's gonna be okay. But the result is, it's pretty good and it's a very, very unusual movie. And when it's about Barbie, it's so benign.

00:52:38.093 --> 00:52:58.946
It's such a simple thing. It's a doll. But then you make this very complex story about it, about death and decay and aging. What I see as a reaction, this people, how they react to the movie, that they like it or completely dislike it, often without knowing anything about it.

00:52:59.974 --> 00:53:21.038
When we talk about it in the Jungian sense, he says people in their personal unconscious have this unconscious forces called a complex. For him, consciousness consists of associations. And when you have the right associations, you can activate this complex, and then the center of a complex is always an emotion. And he calls it really like a mousetrap.

00:53:22.744 --> 00:53:44.852
So with the right words, people get triggered to act in a certain way. It's like a little program that takes over and they just are rattling on about a certain topic. And there are a lot of things mentioned in the movie, feminism and patriarchy and so on. Those are loaded terms by now. So people hear this and they immediately have an opinion.

00:53:45.206 --> 00:54:03.983
They immediately think of something and this clouds the judgment and brings them into think in certain ways and to evaluate in certain ways. And that's what I meant by, by it's for different people, hard to pin down the movie because depending on why you look, you see very different things on a kind of words you react to.

00:54:05.750 --> 00:54:21.444
But all in all, I have to say it's, it's a fun movie and I'm, I'm glad that they did it. And to bring it all around to, to the very beginning of the event, talking about opposites, there's this concept in Hollywood called counter-programming.

00:54:23.600 --> 00:54:44.773
There are countless movies being made every year, and they come out almost every day, new movies constantly. So you have to really think about, okay, when do I show a movie? And when you want to have a big movie, you say like, okay, I do it before Christmas or before holidays because then people have time with their family to go to the cinema and you make a lot of money.

00:54:45.896 --> 00:55:01.291
But you also have to take into account what other movies are being shown. And something very old, a very old trick in Hollywood is to do counter-programming. You make two movies which are very different, almost polar opposites, and show them at the same time.

00:55:01.548 --> 00:55:19.434
So you can grab a bigger market because people saying, oh, and I don't want to do, I don't want to see a romantic movie on Valentine's Day, or I don't want to see a horror movie on Halloween. So there's always another option and you can go there even out of protest. In this case, it's very interesting that it was complementary.

00:55:19.835 --> 00:55:39.206
Yes, you have Oppenheimer and Barbie at the same time and people got completely into it and they saw both movies. Like Oppenheimer is a 3-hour very strangely structured biopic about the inventor of the atom bomb, and it's now the third most successful movie of the year.

00:55:39.351 --> 00:56:01.517
It, I guess it really took an advantage and got a huge advantage through this marketing with Barbie. But it's no coincidence that people like this Oppenheimer. It's the opposite again, and people like this to have the contrast because the opposites and the contrast, this is how we see and we experience.

00:56:01.565 --> 00:56:12.859
